// `const int32& $r` is the read-only borrow, and the doc's recommended form for a parameter
// that only needs to look at its argument (book/concept/pointers_and_refs_v2.md, "Const")
//
// it was unusable. `&$a` yields `int32&`, and implicit convertibility only ever dropped const at
// the *top* level, so a mutable borrow did not satisfy a const one and every call site failed
// with "cannot implicitly convert 'int32&' to 'const int32&'". adding const to the pointee is
// safe - promising only to read is weaker than being allowed to write - so it is now allowed,
// while removing it still is not

function show(const int& $r) : void {
    echo $r;
}

$a = 5;

// the implicit borrow at the call site, and the explicit address, both satisfy it
show($a);
show(&$a);

// a mutable borrow converts to the read-only form
int& $r = &$a;
show($r);

// a nullable pointer does not, though: the const is independent of the nullability, and
// narrowing to a non-null borrow still has to be written out
ptr<int> $p = &$a;
show(int&($p:$));

// a const borrow local reads through exactly like a mutable one
const int& $view = &$a;
echo $view;

// and it follows the storage: it is a borrow, not a copy
$a = 9;
echo $view;

// the const is on the pointee, so the two spellings remain distinct types even though one
// converts to the other
echo ($view:$ == $r:$);
